• Field_4 (64,53–80,76; needs 6): Six drones are already protecting Field_4 (Drones 4, 5, 8, 10, 19, 23). No addition is necessary.
• Field_5 (32,49–56,72; needs 9): Nine drones are already protecting Field_5 (Drones 1, 2, 3, 11, 12, 16, 18, 20, 21). Fully protected.
• Field_6 (2,39–10,62; needs 3): Three drones are protecting Field_6 (Drones 6, 14, 24), though we note this field’s threat level is very low (0.07).
• Field_7 (13,41–29,49; needs 2): No drones assigned.
• Field_8 (64,5–71,12; needs 1): No drones assigned.
• Field_9 (40,30–48,45; needs 2): No drones assigned.
• Field_10 (4,7–28,15; needs 3): Two drones protecting (Drones 7, 13) plus one drone moving (Drone_17) yield a full team.
• Field_11 (53,2–60,18; needs 2): One drone (Drone_9) is protecting; so 1 more is needed.
• Field_12 (2,76–9,83; needs 1): One drone is moving (Drone_22) so that field is covered.

Step 2 – Decide which fields truly need protection:
There are not enough drones to fully cover every field. In deciding where full protection (meaning the allocated number exactly meets the “for full protection” requirement) makes a difference – only fully covered fields will have the birds flee. We therefore concentrate the available drones on fields with higher bird-threat levels. The threat levels are:
 Field_4: 0.43, Field_10: 0.33, Field_5: 0.29, Field_11: 0.23, Field_12: 0.21, Field_2: 0.20, Field_1: 0.19, Field_7: 0.19, then Field_3: 0.12, Field_6: 0.07, Field_8: 0.07, Field_9: 0.05.
We decide that the fields with the highest threat – and/or where there is already a drone in motion – should be fully protected. In our plan we choose to guarantee full protection for Field_4, Field_5, Field_10, and Field_12 (which already are complete) and then for one extra field among those with a threat around 0.19 plus for Field_11 (0.23). There is not enough “drones budget” (24 total) to cover all fields requiring protection. In our allocation we “sacrifice” the very low‐threat fields (Field_2, Field_7, Field_8, Field_9 and Field_6) and instead reassign drones originally scheduled for a low-threat zone (Field_6, which is very over‐protected relative to its threat) to higher‐threat fields that need additional drones.
Thus we choose to protect fully these fields:
 – Field_1 (threat 0.19) because it already has one moving drone;
 – Field_11 (threat 0.23) by adding one extra drone;
 – Field_3 (although its threat is only 0.12, it needs 1 drone and releasing one from a low‐priority Field_6 allows full coverage).
The remaining fields (Field_2, Field_7, Field_8, Field_9, and even Field_6) will not receive any drones. Partial protection is not effective.

Step 3 – Retain the drones already protecting or moving toward fields that will be protected:
• Field_4: Keep Drones 4, 5, 8, 10, 19, 23.
• Field_5: Keep Drones 1, 2, 3, 11, 12, 16, 18, 20, 21.
• Field_10: Keep Drones 7, 13 and Drone 17 (moving).
• Field_12: Keep Drone 22 (moving).
• Field_1: Already has Drone 15 (moving). It needs one more.
• Field_11: Already has Drone 9; it needs one more.
• Field_3: It needs its one drone.
The drones originally protecting Field_6 – Drones 6, 14, and 24 – come from a very low-threat field. We decide to reassign them to fields that need extra protection. We choose to send:
 – Drone 6 to Field_1 (to complete its required 2).
 – Drone 14 to Field_11 (to complete its required 2).
 – Drone 24 to Field_3 (to complete its required 1).

Step 4 – Assign additional drones from the nearest (or available) ones:
Based on the above reasoning, we reassign the drones from Field_6 (low-threat) to high-threat targets needing extra drones. (The choice is guided by the idea that drones already in Field_6 are “close by” relative to making a reallocation decision.) This yields the following additional assignments:
 Field_1: Add Drone 6.
 Field_11: Add Drone 14.
 Field_3: Add Drone 24.
Now the full-protection teams become:
 Field_1: Drone 15 + Drone 6 = 2.
 Field_3: Drone 24 = 1.
 Field_4: Drones 4, 5, 8, 10, 19, 23 = 6.
 Field_5: Drones 1, 2, 3, 11, 12, 16, 18, 20, 21 = 9.
 Field_10: Drones 7, 13, 17 = 3.
 Field_11: Drones 9, 14 = 2.
 Field_12: Drone 22 = 1.
All other fields (Field_2, Field_7, Field_6, Field_8, Field_9) will have no drones (i.e. remain idle).

Step 5 – Final group assignments:
We now give each drone exactly one group. For those drones that are now protecting the selected fields we use the “protecting Field_X” group; the others that have been “reassigned away” from originally protecting a low-threat field now join their new group. (Note: Even though every drone is assigned to a field in our plan, the fields chosen for protection are exactly those we want to have full protection.)

