• Field_5 (req 9): 0 protecting, 0 flying → needs 9.
• Field_6 (req 3): 0 protecting, 3 flying (Drone_3, Drone_19, Drone_23) → full if all reach.
• Field_7 (req 2): 0 protecting, 2 flying (Drone_12, Drone_20) → full if they arrive.
• Field_8 (req 1): 0 protecting, 0 flying → needs 1 (but threat is zero).
• Field_9 (req 2): 0 protecting, 1 flying (Drone_8) → needs 1.
• Field_10 (req 3): 3 protecting (Drone_17, Drone_22, Drone_24), 0 flying → full.
• Field_11 (req 2): 0 protecting, 2 flying (Drone_4, Drone_5) → full.
• Field_12 (req 1): 0 protecting, 1 flying (Drone_10) → full if that drone goes in time.

Step 2 – Decide which fields “need protection” given limited drones and the birds’ threat levels. Note that available drones total 24 are already allocated to groups except for the fields that presently have no “assigned” drone. In order of descending threat level the fields are:
 • Field_4 (0.27), Field_2 (0.12) and Field_7 (0.12) – already have the full complement.
 • Then fields with threat around 0.09: Field_5 (but needing 9, which is too many to cover with our limited fleet), Field_9 (needs 1), and Field_11 (already covered).
 • Next, threat 0.08: Field_3 (needs 1) and Field_10 (fully covered).
 • The remaining (Field_1: 0.06, Field_6: 0.06, Field_12: 0.05, Field_8: 0.0) are lower.
We therefore decide that among fields not yet “covered” we emphasize filling the small missing spots in Field_9 and Field_3 while (given the shortage) leaving the very high‐demand Field_5 and the low‐threat Field_1, Field_8 unprotected. (Field_6 and Field_12 already have a “full” assignment – although note these assignments come from drones in transit.)

Step 3 – Look at the current assignments:
• The “locked” groups already meeting their full‐requirements are for Field_2, Field_4, Field_7, Field_10, and Field_11.
• Field_6 has three drones assigned (Drone_3, Drone_19, Drone_23) but its threat (0.06) is lower than that of Field_9 (0.09) and Field_3 (0.08) so we consider if a sacrifice is justified.
• Field_9 has one drone (Drone_8) but needs one more.
• Field_3 has no drone assigned and needs one.

Step 4 – Reassign drones so that the most threatened “small‐slot” fields are protected. We cannot “add” extra drones because every drone is already assigned. We must pick a drone from a group that is exactly meeting its requirement and “sacrifice” that group’s full protection from a lower‐threat field. Comparing the fields that are exactly full, we see that Field_6 (threat 0.06, full with three moving drones) and Field_12 (threat 0.05, with one flying drone) are the least critical. In order to cover the fields with a slightly higher threat (Field_9 at 0.09 and Field_3 at 0.08) we do the following:
• Remove one drone from Field_6’s group (choosing the one closest in position to Field_9 – here we pick Drone_23) so that Field_9 now gets a second drone and becomes fully protected.
• Remove the single drone originally assigned to Field_12 (Drone_10) and reassign it to Field_3 so that Field_3 is fully protected.
As a result, Field_6 will have 2 drones (falling short of its full complement by 1) and Field_12 will have 0. We accept a slight “underserving” of these lower‐threat fields in favor of preventing more damage in Field_9 and Field_3.

