Step 2. Decide which fields to actively assign protection.
Because we have limited drones we prefer fields with higher bird-threat:
– High-threat fields: Field_7 (threat 0.13), Field_2 (0.12), Field_5 (0.11), Field_8 (0.10), Field_12 (0.10), Field_9 (0.09).
These are already fully covered except Field_7.
– The remaining fields (Field_1: 0.06, Field_6: 0.05, and Field_3, Field_4, Field_10, Field_11 with threat 0.04/0.03) are lower‐priority.
Thus we focus on ensuring Field_7 becomes fully protected. We note that Field_3, Field_4, Field_10, and Field_11 remain unprotected—but their low threat level means we can tolerate them remaining without drones.

Step 3. For each field that needs protection, we keep the drones already on their way.
Field_7 needs 2 drones but currently has no drones assigned. Field_7 is the single high–threat field in need. All others (Field_1, Field_2, Field_5, Field_8, Field_9, Field_12) are already covered.

Step 4. Assign drones to fill protection gaps:
We now must “hire” 2 drones for Field_7. We have no idle drones (none are in state idle) so we must reassign some from fields whose risk is low. The idea is to sacrifice protection in lower–threat fields to cover Field_7.
Among currently fully protected fields, the ones with lowest threat are Field_6 (threat 0.05) and Field_1 (threat 0.06). We compare:
– Field_6 currently has exactly 3 drones protecting, but its threat (0.05) is even lower than Field_1 (0.06). Therefore, it is less “costly” to lose protection over Field_6.
We choose to reassign 2 drones from Field_6 to cover Field_7.
Within Field_6, we decide to remove the drones that are best positioned to reach Field_7 quickly. Calculating approximate distances from Field_6 (rectangle: x between 2 and 10; y between 39 and 62) to Field_7 (rectangle: x between 13 and 29; y between 41 and 49), we note:
• Drone_3 is at (6,43) → estimated distance ~15.
• Drone_8 is at (6,51) → estimated distance ~16.
• Drone_23 is at (6,59) → estimated to be further.
We thus reassign Drone_3 and Drone_8 from Field_6 to Field_7.
This leaves Field_6 with Drone_23 only (1 drone against a requirement of 3) but its low threat (0.05) makes this sacrifice acceptable compared to leaving Field_7 (threat 0.13) unprotected.

Step 5. Final group assignments.
We list each drone with its new group. Drones originally on a field that remain unchanged keep their assignment. Drones reallocated change to the “protecting Field_7” group. The allowed groups are exactly as specified.

